#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""RF Pi offline voice translator for phone (voice) modes.

Speak your own language into the headset. The Pi transcribes it, translates
it, and speaks the other operator's language into the radio (English plus
29 partner languages; see LANGUAGES). Incoming
transmissions run the same chain in reverse into your headphones, with the
original transcript and the translation on screen. Nothing leaves the Pi:
speech-to-text (whisper.cpp), translation (Argos Translate) and text-to-
speech (Piper) all run locally, with no internet.

Concept by David P. Eisenhardt, Jr. (W4DPE).

  hampi-translate check                  verify engines, packs, voices, audio devices, rigctld
  hampi-translate run                    operate: local PTT -> translate -> TX; RX -> headphones
  hampi-translate run --no-tx            practice: translated audio goes to your headphones
  hampi-translate text "Good morning"    translate typed text (--speak, --out x.wav, --rx,
                                         --from/--to LANG)
  hampi-translate file in.wav            translate a recording (--out x.wav, --rx)
  hampi-translate bench [in.wav]         time each stage on this board
  hampi-translate devices                list audio devices
  hampi-translate fetch-packs --langs it translation packs (the one step needing internet)

Config: /etc/hampi/translate.conf. The callsign comes from /etc/hampi/station.conf.
Models: sudo rfpi-translator-install (once, needs internet; it is
scripts/install-translator.sh in the repo).

Keying policy (same as the rest of RF Pi: keying stays a physical act). The
radio is keyed only as the direct result of the operator's own push-to-talk
press, and with REVIEW=1 only after a second confirming press (the "i" ID
command sends the callsign directly). No timer, network request or dashboard
button can key the transmitter.
"""

